{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"acb1a48bd836d5ee9","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01NuaRrQBy9LjiJiC6cyTxBs","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-5RvejA/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-5RvejA-translated-rust/f3474c86-67f7-4839-840d-8be73b7aa864/tasks/acb1a48bd836d5ee9.output","summary":"## Verdict\n\n**I could not find a single f64 for which `format_f64_1(v)` differs from glibc's `printf(\"%.1f\", v)`.** I attacked it two ways — a proof, and ~280 M values of differential fuzzing against real glibc 2.34 — and the function is exactly correct on every input, including all the categories you listed. Below is the evidence, the *two* load-bearing facts that make it correct (one of which contradicts the comment in the code), and 58 concrete ranked regression candidates with bit patterns.\n\nFiles: `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-5RvejA/translated_rust/src/imp.rs` (lines 51-80), `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-5RvejA/translated_rust/c_src/src/main.c` (line 45).\n\n## Why it is correct\n\n**glibc side.** `__printf_fp` rounds the *exact* binary value using `round_away()` (`include/rounding-mode.h`): for `FE_TONEAREST` it is `half_bit && (last_digit_odd || more_bits)` — i.e. round-half-to-**even**. Confirmed empirically (`0.25 -> \"0.2\"`, `0.75 -> \"0.8\"`, `2.25 -> \"2.2\"`).\n\n**Rust side (read from the actual toolchain, 1.94.0, no `rust-toolchain` pin in the repo).** `{:.1}` → `float_to_decimal_common_exact` → `flt2dec::to_exact_fixed_str(grisu::format_exact, ...)`. Grisu's `possibly_round` returns `None` on any tie or near-tie (`/home/scheschb/.rustup/toolchains/1.94.0-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/.../flt2dec/strategy/grisu.rs:714,736`), so all interesting cases fall through to the exact bignum Dragon4, whose rounding step is:\n\n```rust\n// dragon.rs:363-370\nlet order = mant.cmp(scale.mul_small(5));\nif order == Ordering::Greater\n    || (order == Ordering::Equal && len > 0 && buf[len - 1].assume_init() & 1 == 1)\n```\n\nThat is **round-half-to-even on the exact binary value** — identical to glibc. So the fallback `format!(\"{:.1}\", v)` never diverges.\n\n> **KEY FINDING (documentation bug, not a behaviour bug).** The comment at `imp.rs:68-70` — *\"Rust's `{:.1}` rounds half away from zero … handle the tie case with round-half-to-even like glibc\"* — is **factually wrong on both counts**. Rust rounds half-to-**even**, and the fast path cannot handle ties at all: a 1-decimal tie means `20·v` is odd, which forces `v = odd/4`, so `v*10.0` is `N+0.5` and `scaled == scaled.trunc()` is *false* (or `|scaled| ≥ 2^53`). Every tie in the range that matters is decided by `format!(\"{:.1}\")`, not by the fast path. The code is right for a reason its own comment denies; if Rust's `{:.1}` ever changed to half-away-from-zero, candidates #13-#26 below would all break and the fast path would not save them. This is the single most important thing to add as a regression test.\n\n**Fast path proof.** Let `N = fl(10·v)` be an integer with `|N| < 2^53`. Since `|N| < 2^53`, `ulp(N) ≤ 1`, and IEEE round-to-nearest gives `|10·v_exact − N| ≤ ulp(N)/2 ≤ 0.5`. If `< 0.5`, `N` is the correctly rounded value. If `= 0.5`, then `ulp(N) = 1`, so `N ∈ [2^52, 2^53)` where the double's mantissa *is* `N`, so the hardware's ties-to-even picks the even integer — exactly what glibc's decimal half-even picks. Hence the fast path is always correct, and the `< 9.007199254740992e15` (= 2^53) guard is precisely the right cut. `(scaled.abs() as u64)` is exact under that guard (no saturation), and `-0.0` is handled by the `scaled == 0.0 && v.is_sign_negative()` clause (needed, since `-0.0 < 0.0` is false).\n\n**Fuzzing.** ~280 M values, 0 fast-path mismatches (80 M+ fast-path firings): all `k/10 ± 1 ULP` for k ≤ 2e6; all `k/4, k/8, …, k/128`; ±3000 doubles around every power of two and their /2,/4,/8,/16; 800 K consecutive doubles straddling `2^53/10`; 20 M consecutive doubles from 450359962737049.5 upward (the `10v ∈ [2^52,2^53)` half-ULP-tie binade); 40 M exact-tie cases `v = odd/4` with `10v ∈ [2^52,2^53)`; 80 M random bit patterns. With the `2^53` guard *removed*, the same corpus produced **11 150 320** mismatches — the guard is doing real work.\n\nAlso verified no-divergence for the exact digit expansion of huge values: `estimate_max_buf_len(exp) = 21 + (5·exp >> 4)` (mod.rs:490) is 324 for `f64::MAX` (`Decoded.exp = 970`) vs 310 digits requested, and 315 for 1e300 vs 302 requested — so Dragon4 never truncates-and-rounds, it emits the exact expansion then zero-fills, matching glibc byte for byte.\n\n## Ranked candidates (Rust literal / bit pattern / glibc string)\n\n### Tier 1 — latent: only the `< 2^53` guard prevents divergence (highest value as regression tests)\nRust today prints the glibc string via the fallback; the \"formula\" column is what the fast-path body *would* print if the guard were widened, dropped, or reordered.\n\n| # | value | bits | glibc `%.1f` | formula if guard lost |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `900719925474099.5` | `0x430999999999999C` | `900719925474099.5` | `900719925474099.6` — smallest \\|v\\| where the formula is wrong; `fl(10v)=9007199254740996` |\n| 2 | `900719925474099.875` | `0x430999999999999F` | `900719925474099.9` | `900719925474099.8` |\n| 3 | `900719925474100.125` | `0x43099999999999A1` | `900719925474100.1` | `900719925474100.2` |\n| 4 | `900719925474100.5` | `0x43099999999999A4` | `900719925474100.5` | `900719925474100.4` |\n| 5 | `-900719925474100.5` | `0xC3099999999999A4` | `-900719925474100.5` | `-900719925474100.4` (sign path too) |\n| 6 | `9007199254740991.0` (2^53−1) | `0x433FFFFFFFFFFFFF` | `9007199254740991.0` | `9007199254740990.4` — `10v` loses 4 low bits |\n| 7 | `9007199254740994.0` (2^53+2) | `0x4340000000000001` | `9007199254740994.0` | `9007199254740993.6` |\n| 8 | `2251799813685247.75` | `0x431FFFFFFFFFFFFF` | `2251799813685247.8` | `2251799813685247.6` — tie *and* over the guard |\n| 9 | `-2251799813685247.75` | `0xC31FFFFFFFFFFFFF` | `-2251799813685247.8` | `-2251799813685247.6` |\n| 10 | `1e300` | `0x7E37E43C8800759C` | 303 chars: `1000000000000000052504760255204420248704…386865459400540160.0` | `1844674407370955161.5` (`as u64` saturates) |\n| 11 | `f64::MAX` | `0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF` | 311 chars: `179769313486231570814527423731704356798…404026184124858368.0` | `1844674407370955161.5` — note `v*10.0 = inf` and **`inf == inf.trunc()` is true**, so only the magnitude test stops this; if the two conjuncts were ever reordered/short-circuited differently this is the witness |\n| 12 | `f64::MIN` (`-f64::MAX`) | `0xFFEFFFFFFFFFFFFF` | 312 chars, leading `-` | `-1844674407370955161.5` |\n| 13 | `1e308` | `0x7FE1CCF385EBC8A0` | 311 chars | same hazard |\n| 14 | `900719925474099.25` | `0x430999999999999A` | `900719925474099.2` | `900719925474099.2` — the **unique** double (±) with `fl(10v) == 2^53` exactly; proves `<` vs `<=` at the boundary is *not* load-bearing (both correct, because `10v = 9007199254740992.5` is a tie that both resolve to even) |\n\n### Tier 2 — exact 1-decimal ties, decided by `format!(\"{:.1}\")`; break iff Rust's rounding mode changes to half-away-from-zero (as `imp.rs`'s comment wrongly claims it already is). Ties are exactly the doubles `v = odd/4`.\nStarred entries are the *discriminating* ones (half-even and half-away differ); `*.75` ties round up under both rules and are weaker tests.\n\n| # | value | bits | glibc = Rust | half-away would give |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 15* | `0.25` | `0x3FD0000000000000` | `0.2` | `0.3` |\n| 16* | `-0.25` | `0xBFD0000000000000` | `-0.2` | `-0.3` |\n| 17* | `1.25` | `0x3FF4000000000000` | `1.2` | `1.3` |\n| 18* | `-1.25` | `0xBFF4000000000000` | `-1.2` | `-1.3` |\n| 19* | `2.25` | `0x4002000000000000` | `2.2` | `2.3` |\n| 20* | `3.25` | `0x400A000000000000` | `3.2` | `3.3` |\n| 21* | `4.25` | `0x4011000000000000` | `4.2` | `4.3` |\n| 22* | `8.25` | `0x4020800000000000` | `8.2` | `8.3` |\n| 23* | `1000000.25` | `0x412E848080000000` | `1000000.2` | `1000000.3` |\n| 24* | `900719925474099.25` | `0x430999999999999A` | `900719925474099.2` | `900719925474099.3` — discriminating tie sitting exactly on the guard |\n| 25 | `0.75` | `0x3FE8000000000000` | `0.8` | (same) |\n| 26 | `-0.75` | `0xBFE8000000000000` | `-0.8` | (same) |\n| 27 | `1.75` | `0x3FFC000000000000` | `1.8` | (same) |\n| 28 | `2.75` | `0x4006000000000000` | `2.8` | (same) |\n| 29 | `3.75` | `0x400E000000000000` | `3.8` | (same) |\n| 30 | `8.75` | `0x4021800000000000` | `8.8` | (same) |\n| 31 | `1000000.75` | `0x412E848180000000` | `1000000.8` | (same) |\n| 32 | `1125899906842623.75` | `0x430FFFFFFFFFFFFE` | `1125899906842623.8` | largest-magnitude tie handled by the fallback |\n\n### Tier 3 — fast path fires *on* an exact tie: correctness depends on IEEE ties-to-even in `v*10.0` agreeing with decimal half-even (40 M of these fuzzed, all match)\n| # | value | bits | glibc = Rust | note |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 33 | `450359962737049.75` | `0x42F999999999999C` | `450359962737049.8` | `10v` exactly `4503599627370497.5` → `fl` = `…498`; the maximal `\\|10v−fl\\|=0.5` case |\n| 34 | `-450359962737049.75` | `0xC2F999999999999C` | `-450359962737049.8` | |\n| 35 | `450359962737050.25` | `0x42F99999999999A4` | `450359962737050.2` | tie resolved *down* to even by hardware |\n| 36 | `562949953421311.75` | `0x42FFFFFFFFFFFFFC` | `562949953421311.8` | |\n| 37 | `562949953421312.25` | `0x4300000000000002` | `562949953421312.2` | |\n| 38 | `900719925474098.75` | `0x4309999999999996` | `900719925474098.8` | largest-magnitude tie that still takes the fast path |\n| 39 | `-900719925474098.75` | `0xC309999999999996` | `-900719925474098.8` | |\n| 40 | `500000000000000.25` | `0x42FC6BF526340004` | `500000000000000.2` | |\n| 41 | `600000000000000.75` | `0x43010D9316EC0006` | `600000000000000.8` | |\n| 42 | `900719925474099.125` | `0x4309999999999999` | `900719925474099.1` | **extreme of the fast path**: `fl(10v) = 2^53−1`, the largest value the guard admits |\n\n### Tier 4 — zero / sign\n| # | value | bits | glibc = Rust | note |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 43 | `-0.0` | `0x8000000000000000` | `-0.0` | fast path; the `scaled == 0.0 && v.is_sign_negative()` clause is load-bearing (`-0.0 < 0.0` is false). Fallback would also give `-0.0` on Rust ≥ 1.53 (`Sign::Minus`, mod.rs:296-323); edition 2021 forces ≥ 1.56, so the pre-1.53 `\"0.0\"` divergence is unreachable |\n| 44 | `0.0` | `0x0000000000000000` | `0.0` | |\n| 45 | `-0.04` | `0xBFA47AE147AE147B` | `-0.0` | negative value rounding to zero, via fallback `FullDecoded::Zero`-style path |\n| 46 | `-5e-324` | `0x8000000000000001` | `-0.0` | min subnormal, sign preserved |\n| 47 | `5e-324` | `0x0000000000000001` | `0.0` | |\n| 48 | `-2.225073858507201e-308` (max subnormal) | `0x800FFFFFFFFFFFFF` | `-0.0` | |\n| 49 | `f64::MIN_POSITIVE` | `0x0010000000000000` | `0.0` | |\n| 50 | `-1e-300` | `0x81A56E1FC2F8F359` | `-0.0` | |\n| 51 | `-0.049999999999999996` / `-0.05` | `0xBFA9999999999999` / `0xBFA999999999999A` | `-0.0` / `-0.1` | adjacent pair straddling the 0.05 boundary (the `-0.05` double is *above* 0.05, so it rounds away) |\n\n### Tier 5 — NaN / Inf (the special-cases in `imp.rs` are load-bearing: bare Rust prints `NaN`, capital)\n| # | value | bits | glibc = Rust |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 52 | `f64::NAN` | `0x7FF8000000000000` | `nan` (Rust `{:.1}` alone would print `NaN`) |\n| 53 | `-f64::NAN` | `0xFFF8000000000000` | `-nan` — `is_sign_negative()` correctly mirrors `signbit`; a `v < 0.0` test would have failed here |\n| 54 | quiet NaN w/ payload | `0x7FF8000000000001` | `nan` (payload ignored by both) |\n| 55 | signalling NaN | `0x7FF0000000000001` / `0xFFF0000000000001` | `nan` / `-nan` |\n| 56 | `f64::INFINITY` / `NEG_INFINITY` | `0x7FF0000000000000` / `0xFFF0000000000000` | `inf` / `-inf` |\n\n### Tier 6 — near-ties / one-ULP neighbours (both sides must agree which side of `.x5` the exact binary value lies on)\n| # | value | bits | glibc = Rust |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 57 | `nextafter(0.25, 0.0)` | `0x3FCFFFFFFFFFFFFF` | `0.2` |\n| 58 | `nextafter(0.25, INF)` | `0x3FD0000000000001` | `0.3` |\n| 59 | `nextafter(-0.25, -INF)` | `0xBFD0000000000001` | `-0.3` |\n| 60 | `0.15` | `0x3FC3333333333333` | `0.1` (double is below 0.15) |\n| 61 | `0.35` | `0x3FD6666666666666` | `0.3` |\n| 62 | `0.45` | `0x3FDCCCCCCCCCCCCD` | `0.5` (double is above 0.45) |\n| 63 | `8.95` | `0x4021E66666666666` | `8.9` |\n| 64 | `2.675` | `0x4005666666666666` | `2.7` |\n| 65 | `0.1` / `nextafter(0.1,0)` / `nextafter(0.1,INF)` | `0x3FB999999999999A` / `…99` / `…9B` | `0.1` for all three — only the middle one takes the fast path (`fl(10v)` is exactly 1); the neighbours give `0.9999999999999999`/`1.0000000000000002`, so the integrality test correctly rejects them |\n| 66 | `2.5`, `3.5`, `4.5` (the actual program values) | `0x4004000000000000`, `0x400C000000000000`, `0x4012000000000000` | `2.5`, `3.5`, `4.5` — fast path, exact |\n\n### Tier 7 — huge magnitudes: fallback must emit the full exact expansion, not a rounded/shortest one\n| # | value | bits | glibc = Rust |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 67 | `1e23` | `0x44B52D02C7E14AF6` | `99999999999999991611392.0` — compact test that `{:.1}` uses *exact* mode, not shortest (`{}` would give `100000000000000000000000`) |\n| 68 | `2f64.powi(1023)` | `0x7FE0000000000000` | 310 chars, `898846567431157953864652595394512366808…112068608.0` |\n| 69 | `1.7976931348623155e308` | `0x7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFE` | 311 chars (one ULP below `f64::MAX`) |\n| 70 | `1e15` / `1e16` / `1e17` / `2^52` | `0x430C6BF526340000` / `0x4341C37937E08000` / `0x4376345785D8A000` / `0x4330000000000000` | `1000000000000000.0` / `10000000000000000.0` / `100000000000000000.0` / `4503599627370496.0` — all just above the guard, all fallback |\n\n## Two environment-dependent divergence classes (not reachable from this program, worth knowing)\n- **Locale.** glibc's `%.1f` uses `LC_NUMERIC`'s decimal point; Rust always emits `.`. `main.c` never calls `setlocale`, so the C program stays in the `\"C\"` locale regardless of env vars — no divergence. (Relevant if `tests/env_diff.rs` ever sets `LC_ALL=de_DE.UTF-8` *and* the C side gains a `setlocale` call.)\n- **FP rounding mode.** glibc's `printf` honours the current mode via `round_away`; Rust's `flt2dec` is hard-wired to half-even. Under `fesetround(FE_UPWARD)`, glibc prints `0.1` for `0.04` while Rust prints `0.0`. Neither program calls `fesetround`, so unreachable.\n\n## Incidental observation\nBecause `format!(\"{:.1}\", v)` is byte-exact against glibc for **every** finite value including `±0.0`, the entire fast path (`imp.rs:71-78`) is behaviourally redundant on Rust ≥ 1.53 — it is a pure optimisation that carries the `2^53` / `as u64` hazard documented in Tier 1. I made no changes to the repo.","usage":{"total_tokens":108107,"tool_uses":25,"duration_ms":1250846},"uuid":"d3504d85-4379-4fe2-9f78-30a427d12109","session_id":"f3474c86-67f7-4839-840d-8be73b7aa864"}
